      "title": "hook (same condition, same day, 800 km apart)",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "So, why do the same causes keep appearing? Two people have the same treatable condition, diagnosed on the same day. One lives in a capital city. The other lives eight hundred kilometres from the nearest specialist. Think of three things that will differ that year. For each, is it a difference in the condition, or in the circumstances? There's a third kind of difference, and it's where the word inequity lives."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "four-questions",
      "title": "four questions, one method",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Last time, you asked four questions of one group: the data, the interaction, the underlying causes, and the actions. The syllabus asks them of every group, so treat them as a method. You choose your second group from the syllabus's five. Choose with three tests. Can you state a trend? Can you name a mechanism? Can you name an action, and a reason it might fail? Pick the group you can explain, not the one you find most interesting."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 1,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "rural-data",
      "title": "rural and remote: the data",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Let's work rural and remote areas in full. Remoteness measures how far a place is from services and population centres, from major cities, through regional, to remote. The data shows a gradient, not a cliff. Regional is worse than the cities, and remote is worse again. Life expectancy is lower, and deaths from injury and from avoidable causes are higher, especially where treatment is time-critical. It has persisted for years, which suggests a cause that hasn't been removed."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 1,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism",
      "title": "the mechanism: say \"because\"",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Now the mechanism, and the key word is because. Distance means a longer trip to care, and for trauma, heart attack and stroke, the treatment is time-critical, so distance turns directly into deaths. The workforce is thinner, so a service can close when one doctor leaves. Incomes are lower, more jobs carry injury risk, and every trip for care costs fuel and a day's pay. Now let them interact. Distance plus a thin workforce means care is delayed. Delay plus risky work means more injuries arrive late. Low income plus travel cost means follow-up is skipped. Each alone is manageable. Together, they hold the gradient in place."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 1,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "cause",
      "title": "the cause: a decision, not a distance",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "So what underpins it? Distance is a fact, not an injustice. What makes this an inequity is a pattern of decisions: where services, training places and roads and phone towers are put, and health workers trained in cities stay there. Those decisions could be made differently, so the difference is avoidable, unnecessary and unfair, which is the test for an inequity. And that's the third kind of difference from your list. Not the condition, and not just the circumstances, but a decision about where care is placed."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 1,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "actions",
      "title": "actions: what each attacks, and its limit",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Now the actions, and the habit that carries this topic. Label each action by the part of the mechanism it attacks, then give its limit. Outreach and visiting specialists attack distance, but they come and go, so continuity stays weak. Rural training pathways attack the workforce, because doctors who train in the country are more likely to stay, but it takes years, often a decade, to show in the data. Retrieval flights attack time directly, but they're costly, weather-dependent, and do nothing for chronic disease, where most of the burden sits. An action is only judged against the mechanism it targets."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 1,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "other-groups",
      "title": "the other four groups, and the same gates",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "The other groups each have their own mechanism. For socioeconomically disadvantaged people, health rises at every step up the ladder, driven by cost, insecurity and low control. Some migrants arrive healthier than people born here, then lose that edge over time, the healthy migrant effect, as language barriers and an unfamiliar system delay care. For people with disability, health is often poorer for reasons that have nothing to do with the disability itself, partly through diagnostic overshadowing: a new symptom blamed on the disability and never checked. Remember Year 11's six access gates, the ways a service can exist and still be out of reach. Watch which gates each group hits. Different groups, stuck at the same gates."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 1,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "overlap",
      "title": "one population, cut five ways",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "They're five ways of cutting one population, and they overlap. Take an older person with a disability, on a low income, in a remote town. Free specialist care in the city doesn't help if the trip is physically impossible. A cheaper medicine doesn't help if collecting it takes a day of travel. Each barrier removes the workaround the others left open. That's compounding disadvantage, and it's why single-barrier solutions work for single-barrier people."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 1,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "memory",
      "title": "memory",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Two things to remember. Name the mechanism, then judge each action against it. And single-barrier solutions work for single-barrier people."
